                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why would we add a config for a DC-SCM separate from a system/platform?  This is surprising to me.  Do you have enough in a device tree that you can plug into an arbitrary system?  Is this really considered an evaluation board?  What's the story on this?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Please add 'meta-flex: ' as a prefix to all your commits."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex",
                        "email": "babu.chandrasekhar@flex.com",
                        "username": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged. Thanks for your comments."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex",
                        "email": "babu.chandrasekhar@flex.com",
                        "username": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex"
                    },
                    "message": "It is a real DC-SCM. There is a stand-alone mode use with an adapter board for firmware development and customer evaluation, with BMC fully working. DC-SCM not attached with a platform is a commonly used model, in fact that is the advantage of detaching BMC from mainboard design. \n\nI am working on getting the device tree checking into Linux kernel as per your suggestions.\n\nIt is not just an eval board, even tough it is one of the options. The card is used in several different platforms. Some reference platforms (will be open sourcing) and some customer platforms (rebranded). I have published one of the chassis in opencompute web site."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex",
                        "email": "babu.chandrasekhar@flex.com",
                        "username": "babu-chandrasekhar-flex"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the code review. Given the number of changes suggested, I will abandon the commit for now. Will clean up and start a fresh submit after getting the device tree updated in kernel."
                },
